.TH "SSHFWD" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
sshfwd \- sSH tunnels without remembering \-L, \-R and \-D
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
sshfwd [options] HOST [TARGET:]PORT ... [-- ssh options]
sshfwd [options] -R HOST PORT [-- ssh options]
sshfwd [options] -D HOST [PORT] [-- ssh options]
sshfwd --list | --stop ID | --stop all
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
SSH can carry other connections inside it. That is how you reach a web page that listens only on a server's localhost, a database that only a bastion host can see, or your laptop's dev server from a remote machine. The ssh options for it, \fB\-L 127.0.0.1:5433:db.example.com:5432\fR and friends, are easy to get backwards. \fBsshfwd\fR takes the host and the port and builds the options for you.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd k8s-master 8080
tunnel  localhost:8080 to port 8080 on k8s-master
open    http://localhost:8080
Ctrl+C closes it.
.fi
.RE
.PP
It prints the local address to use, and a URL when the port usually speaks HTTP. The tunnel stays open until Ctrl+C. With \fB\-b\fR it keeps running in the background instead, and \fB\-\-list\fR and \fB\-\-stop\fR manage it later.
.SH THE THREE KINDS OF TUNNEL
.SS Local forward, the default
.PP
\fBsshfwd HOST PORT\fR opens a port on your machine that leads to a port on the host. Your browser or client connects to \fBlocalhost:PORT\fR, and ssh carries the connection to the host, where it reaches \fBlocalhost:PORT\fR as seen from there.
.PP
Use it for things that listen only on the server itself, such as a Grafana on \fB127.0.0.1:3000\fR, a Kubernetes dashboard, or a database that refuses outside connections.
.PP
\fBsshfwd HOST TARGET:PORT\fR goes one step further. The host connects on to TARGET, which only has to be reachable from the host. That is the bastion pattern, where a private database is only open to one jump server:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd bastion.example.com db.example.com:5432
tunnel  localhost:5432 to db.example.com:5432
        through bastion.example.com
Ctrl+C closes it.
.fi
.RE
.PP
TARGET is resolved on the host, so private DNS names work even when your machine cannot resolve them. An IPv6 target goes in brackets, such as \fB[fd00::5]:5432\fR.
.PP
Several ports go through one connection: \fBsshfwd k8s\-master 8080 9090 3000\fR.
.SS Remote forward, \-R
.PP
\fBsshfwd \-R HOST PORT\fR is the other way round. It opens PORT on the host, and connections to it come back to PORT on your machine. Use it to show a dev server on your laptop to a remote machine, or to let a server reach a service that runs only on your side.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd -R k8s-master 5173
remote  port 5173 on k8s-master leads to localhost:5173
Ctrl+C closes it.
.fi
.RE
.PP
The port on the host listens on its localhost only, unless the host's sshd has \fBGatewayPorts yes\fR. \fBsshfwd\fR warns when nothing listens on your side of the port yet, since the tunnel then leads nowhere.
.SS SOCKS proxy, \-D
.PP
\fBsshfwd \-D HOST\fR starts a SOCKS proxy on \fB127.0.0.1:1080\fR. Any program that speaks SOCKS then reaches the network as if it ran on the host. It suits a browser that needs to see several internal sites at once.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd -D bastion.example.com
socks   127.0.0.1:1080 through bastion.example.com
try it  curl --socks5-hostname 127.0.0.1:1080 https://example.com
Ctrl+C closes it.
.fi
.RE
.PP
In Firefox, set it under Settings, Network settings, Manual proxy, SOCKS host \fB127.0.0.1\fR, port 1080, SOCKS v5, and tick "Proxy DNS when using SOCKS v5" so internal names resolve on the host.
.SH LOCAL PORTS
.PP
Every local port \fBsshfwd\fR opens listens on \fB127.0.0.1\fR only, so other machines on your network cannot use your tunnel.
.PP
The local port is the same number as the remote one when it is free. \fBsshfwd\fR changes it in two cases and says so:
.TP
The port is taken on your machine
It takes the next free port above it, and names the program holding the taken one.
.TP
The port is below 1024 and you are not root
Only root can open those, so it uses the port plus 8000. Port 80 becomes 8080 and 443 becomes 8443.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
local 5432 is taken by rootlessport (pid 1790), using 5433
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\-l PORT\fR asks for one exact local port. When that port is taken, \fBsshfwd\fR stops with exit 1 rather than take another, since you asked for that one.
.SH BACKGROUND TUNNELS
.PP
\fB\-b\fR leaves the tunnel running after \fBsshfwd\fR returns, and gives it a small number:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd -b bastion.example.com db.example.com:5432
local 5432 is taken by rootlessport (pid 1790), using 5433
tunnel 1  localhost:5433 to db.example.com:5432
          through bastion.example.com
in the background, pid 7012. Close it with: sshfwd --stop 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-list\fR shows the ones still running, and how long each has been up:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd --list
ID  PID      UP     TUNNEL
1   7012     2h     localhost:5433 to db.example.com:5432, through bastion.example.com
2   7240     5m     localhost:8080 to port 8080 on k8s-master
.fi
.RE
.PP
A tunnel whose ssh has died, because the network dropped or the host rebooted, is removed from the list with a note. \fB\-\-stop 1\fR closes one tunnel, and \fB\-\-stop all\fR closes every one.
.PP
\fBsshfwd\fR keeps a record per tunnel in \fB~/.local/state/toolbelt/sshfwd\fR, with the ssh control socket and a log of ssh's messages. The folder is readable only by you. Nothing in it outlives the tunnel.
.SH HOW IT RUNS SSH
.PP
\fBsshfwd\fR runs one ssh command, which \fB\-v\fR prints first:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
ssh -f -N -L 127.0.0.1:8080:localhost:8080 -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes
    -o ServerAliveInterval=30 -o ServerAliveCountMax=3 -o ControlMaster=yes
    -o ControlPath=SOCKET -o ControlPersist=no k8s-master
.fi
.RE
.TP
\fB\-N\fR
Run no command on the host, only carry the tunnel.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Go to the background once logged in and the tunnel is up, so \fBsshfwd\fR knows it worked before it prints the address.
.TP
\fBExitOnForwardFailure=yes\fR
Fail at once when the host refuses the tunnel, instead of logging in with no tunnel.
.TP
\fBServerAliveInterval=30\fR
Check the link every 30 seconds, and give up after three missed checks, so a dead tunnel does not hang for ever.
.TP
\fBControlMaster\fR, \fBControlPath\fR
A control socket, so \fBsshfwd\fR can find the ssh process and close it cleanly with \fBssh \-O exit\fR.
.PP
A password or a key passphrase prompt works as usual, before ssh goes to the background. Your \fB~/.ssh/config\fR applies, so host aliases, \fBUser\fR, \fBIdentityFile\fR, \fBProxyJump\fR and \fBPort\fR lines all work.
.PP
In the foreground, \fBsshfwd\fR checks every second that ssh is still running. When the tunnel drops, it says so and exits 1. Ctrl+C closes the tunnel and exits 0.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-remote\fR
Open PORT on the host, leading back to PORT on your machine.
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-socks\fR
A SOCKS proxy through the host, on local port 1080 unless you name one.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-local PORT\fR
Use this local port. With several forwards, it is refused, since one number cannot fit all.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-background\fR
Keep the tunnel after the command ends.
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
List background tunnels.
.TP
\fB\-\-stop ID\fR
Close a background tunnel. \fB\-\-stop all\fR closes every one.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print nothing but errors. With \fB\-b\fR, print only the tunnel ID, for scripts.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print the ssh command before it runs.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
Options after \fB\-\-\fR go to ssh, before the host:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd k8s-master 8080 -- -i ~/.ssh/lab_ed25519 -p 2222
$ sshfwd db-internal 5432 -- -J bastion.example.com
$ sshfwd k8s-master 8080 -- -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\-J\fR jumps through a bastion first, which is another way to reach a private host.
.SH NEEDS
.PP
\fBssh\fR from OpenSSH, 6.7 or newer for the control socket commands. It comes from \fBopenssh\-client\fR on Debian and Ubuntu, \fBopenssh\-clients\fR on Fedora, \fBopenssh\fR on Arch, and \fBopenssh\-client\fR on Alpine. \fBss\fR from iproute2 names the program on a taken port, and without it the message says "another program".
.PP
The host's sshd must allow forwarding. It does by default. When \fBAllowTcpForwarding no\fR is set, every tunnel fails.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS A dashboard that listens only on the server
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd k8s-master 8080
tunnel  localhost:8080 to port 8080 on k8s-master
open    http://localhost:8080
Ctrl+C closes it.
.fi
.RE
.SS A database behind a bastion, in the background
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd -b bastion.example.com db.example.com:5432
tunnel 1  localhost:5432 to db.example.com:5432
          through bastion.example.com
in the background, pid 7012. Close it with: sshfwd --stop 1
$ psql -h localhost -p 5432 app
.fi
.RE
.SS A tunnel in a script
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
id=$(sshfwd -q -b bastion.example.com db.example.com:5432) || exit 1
pg_dump -h localhost app > app.sql
sshfwd --stop "$id"
.fi
.RE
.SS A privileged port
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd -b k8s-master 22 8080
local 22 needs root, using 8022
tunnel 2  localhost:8022 to port 22 on k8s-master
          localhost:8080 to port 8080 on k8s-master
in the background, pid 7240. Close it with: sshfwd --stop 2
.fi
.RE
.SS A SOCKS proxy, with the ssh command
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd -v -D bastion.example.com
+ ssh -f -N -D 127.0.0.1:1080 -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes -o ServerAliveInterval=30 ...
socks   127.0.0.1:1080 through bastion.example.com
try it  curl --socks5-hostname 127.0.0.1:1080 https://example.com
Ctrl+C closes it.
.fi
.RE
.SS A key the host does not accept
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ sshfwd k8s-worker-1 9090
sshfwd: ssh to k8s-worker-1 failed. Permission denied (publickey).
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
\fBPermission denied (publickey)\fR : The host does not accept your key. Check you can log in with \fBssh HOST\fR first, and pass the right key with \fB\-\- \-i KEY\fR.
.PP
\fBremote port forwarding failed\fR or \fBadministratively prohibited\fR : The host's sshd does not allow tunnels, or \fB\-R\fR asked for a port that is taken on the host. Ask the admin about \fBAllowTcpForwarding\fR, or pick another port.
.PP
The tunnel opens but the page does not load : The service on the host may listen on another address than \fBlocalhost\fR, such as only the host's LAN IP. Name that address as the target: \fBsshfwd HOST 10.0.0.5:8080\fR.
.PP
\fBthe tunnel to HOST dropped\fR : The network went away, the host rebooted, or the laptop slept. Start it again. For tunnels that must come back by themselves, use \fBautossh\fR or a systemd unit.
.PP
\fB\-\-list\fR shows nothing but the tunnel still works : It was started without \fB\-b\fR, or by plain ssh. Only background tunnels from \fBsshfwd\fR get a record.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
The tunnel opened, and closed on Ctrl+C. Or the list or stop worked.
.TP
1
ssh failed, the tunnel dropped, the \fB\-l\fR port is taken, or the tunnel ID does not exist.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as no port, a bad port number, or \fB\-R\fR with \fB\-D\fR.
.TP
3
\fBssh\fR is missing.
.TP
143
sshfwd itself was stopped with a TERM signal. It closes the tunnel first.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBport\fR, \fBwaitfor\fR, \fBssh(1)\fR, \fBssh_config(5)\fR
